  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.5. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement labour market programmes and employment services for transitions to decent work over the life course, with particular focus on young and older workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Federal Service for Labour and Employment (ROSTRUD) substantially revised its institutional framework and implemented a federal reform on employment services and labour market programmes in line with up-to-date international standards and practices, including inter-institutional measures to improve services addressing transitions to decent work.

A federal training plan was agreed and rolled out for improving the regional employment centres, its organizational structure, management, work processes and methodologies, in response to evolving labour market and institutional needs identified by the federal reform.

As part of the reform, ROSTRUD launched the federal training plan for the large-scale staff development programme for over 4,000 staff in June 2021. The training content is based on the manual &quot;Public Employment Services and the Future of Work: Training Manual for the CIS Regional Network for Youth Employment Promotion&quot;, validated by a CIS subregional PES network during subregional PES trainings in 2020. 

ROSTRUD adapted its employment services and programmes to the COVID-19 job crisis, and submitted for tripartite discussion the monitoring reports prepared with ILO technical support.

Employment centres coverage increased by twice from January to December 2020. Institutional strengths built during 2020, were reinforced with staff&apos;s skills development activities implemented during 2021.

In three regions of the Russian Federation &#x2013; Komi Republic, Astrakhan Region, and Kaliningrad Region &#x2013; the employment service prioritized in tripartite discussions, designed and launched pilot active labour market programmes targeting young people, covering flagship youth programmes such as wage subsidies and on-the-job-trainings modalities for people not in education, employment or training (NEETs), career guidance services and youth entrepreneurship for rural development. The ILO provided technical support for the implementation of the federal PES reform providing advice, benchmarking against international standards, and sharing and disseminating best international practices, mainly from European and East European neighbour countries.

For the federal PES reform, the ILO developed a training Manual on PES and the Future of Work, which provided guidance for a comprehensive staff capacity-building plan in response to the institutional needs identified in interregional employment centres meetings held in 2019. The Manual was tested and validated during on-line PES trainings in 2020-21, and finally ROSTRUD adopted it as a training tool for the large-scale staff development programme launched in mid-2021. 

The ILO prepared and disseminated systematic monitoring reports on COVID-19 crisis response, submitted to tripartite discussions, in 7 webinars from June to December 2020, and prepared a final report on lessons learnt of the Russian Federation response to the COVID-19 employment crisis, with focus on the role played by the employment centres and on the employment services and programs for youth.

With the ILO&apos;s technical support, tailor-made pilot active labour market programmes were designed and launched at regional level, and the ILO facilitated peer-to-peer policy reviews, collaboration and mutual learning activities between Russian regions. The ILO facilitated meetings with regional stakeholders, which helped to identify and select strategic areas and potential partners. The regions developed a promising approach to public-private partnerships in terms of expanding work-based opportunities for young people, working jointly with educational institutions and private enterprises to increase the effectiveness of services for young people (areas covered were: job-placements, career guidance, first work experience programs, internships or traineeships at enterprises and youth entrepreneurship).</narrative>
   </description>
